--TEST--
an entry `Core\Cache::process()` holds outlives the request that wrote it: the isolate a `spawn` runs reads what the parent put there, and the parent reads what that isolate wrote after it has finished — `rule:concurrency/the-process-tier-is-one-store-per-process` and `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`: `spawn script` asks for
# `script.spawn` at its own door, and deny-by-default means a case with no
# configuration cannot spawn at all. The tier itself is granted nothing, which is
# the asymmetry `nvs_stdlib::registry`'s capability table states beside
# `Core\Cache::shared`.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
// A later request, in the sense that decides this: its own isolate, its own
// heap and its own statics, sharing with the parent only what
// `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing` lets it share. The process tier is one
// of those things and the per-core tier is not — whichever core runs this
// isolate is the scheduler's business, so a case reading `local()` here would be
// a case about scheduling.
var $cache = Core\Cache::process();
echo "the next request reads ", $cache->get("written-before-the-spawn") as string, "\n";

// Written for whoever runs next, which is the parent: this isolate is finished
// by the time that read happens, so nothing holding the entry belongs to a
// request any more.
$cache->put("written-by-the-child", "the-childs-value");
return "done";
--FILE--
<?nvs
var $cache = Core\Cache::process();
$cache->put("written-before-the-spawn", "the-parents-value");

var $job = spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
var $done = await $job;
echo "ok=", $done->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo $done->output;

// The direction that makes this a store rather than an inheritance: the entry
// was written by an isolate that no longer exists, and it is read here through a
// handle taken before that isolate started.
echo "and reads what it left behind: ", $cache->get("written-by-the-child") as string, "\n";

// A miss is an ordinary answer on this tier for every reason it is one on the
// per-core tier, and for one more: this may be a different process than the one
// that wrote the entry.
echo "a name nothing wrote: ", $cache->get("never-written") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
--EXPECT--
ok=true
the next request reads the-parents-value
and reads what it left behind: the-childs-value
a name nothing wrote: null
